  "id": "urn:uuid:4a86e515-312d-4718-a18f-a772c2bf033e",
  "courseCode": "702",
  "name": {
    "en": "Pentesting with AI?"
  },
  "description": [
    {
      "type": "TextObject",
      "inLanguage": "en",
      "text": "\u003Cp\u003EA hands-on introduction to finding vulnerabilities in web apps and to how AI agents are reshaping both attack and defense. We will break three CTF challenges on OWASP Juice Shop by hand, then compare how AI pentesting agents tackle the same targets. Covers red, blue and purple teaming, recent incidents, and small-scale automation. Ethics and rules of engagement up front; all work on sanctioned, self-hosted targets only.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch3\u003ELearning Goals:\u003C/h3\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPlanning and running security tests within a sanctioned scope and agreed rules of engagement.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFinding and exploiting vulnerabilities by hand in a deliberately vulnerable web app like OWASP Juice Shop.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EApplying open-source AI pentesting agents and comparing their findings with the manual approach.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAssessing what AI agents contribute to red, blue and purple teaming, using concrete incidents.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECarrying out a pentesting project of their own and documenting the findings in a vulnerability report.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Ch3\u003EWhat you get\u003C/h3\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThe course instruction is hybrid, which means you can participate either online or in person in Kiel (see location listed in the dates).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach challenge follows the same rhythm: you attack our deliberately vulnerable Juiceshop web-app by hand first, we discuss and show-and-tell what everyone found, and only then, once thoroughly understood, will we discuss AI/LLM-based approaches against the same target.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou get your own access to a safe-to-attack CTF environment (self-hosted, network-isolated), reachable via VPN, plus an introduction to current open-source AI pentesting agents/frameworks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe course will offer real-world context and a capstone project where you apply what you've learned end to end.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll needed software is free and open-source.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch3\u003EWhat you should bring\u003C/h3\u003E\u003Cp\u003EAll tests stays inside the course's defined, sanctioned scope — you'll sign a commitment to the rules of engagement up front.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou don't need prior pentesting experience, but you should be comfortable with a computer: running a terminal, installing software (git, Docker, a VPN client, SSH), and getting Docker and a VPN client running on your own machine.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should bring sufficient time and a willingness to show your work, including dead ends: the format runs on show-and-tell, not polished results or AI slop. We are as interested in this topic as you are. Between sessions you'll work on CTF homework challenges. Expect a written note after each manual challenge (what you found, how, how long it took, what dead ends you hit) — this is what makes the later manual-vs-AI comparison possible, and doubles as practice for writing the vulnerability report.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn order to receive a certificate of achievement (Leistungszertifikat / 2.5 ECTS) for this course, active participation is expected, and you have to conduct a practice project and submit a finished report. \u003C/p\u003E",
      "encodingFormat": "text/html"
    }
  ],
